ITEMS = [
("A policy has NAT enabled with no IP pool. What source IP do outgoing packets get?",
 ["The egress interface IP", "The ingress interface IP", "The management port IP", "The FortiGate loopback IP"], "A",
 "With Use Outgoing Interface Address, FortiGate rewrites the source to the IP of the interface the packet leaves through."),
("Which IP pool type is the default, letting many hosts share addresses through port translation?",
 ["One-to-one", "Overload", "Fixed port range", "Port block allocation"], "B",
 "Overload is the default IP pool type: many internal hosts share the pool's addresses, kept apart by source port translation. One-to-one gives each host its own external IP."),
("A one-to-one IP pool has 10 addresses, all in use. What happens to an eleventh host?",
 ["It shares the first IP", "It uses the interface IP", "Its session fails", "It gets a random port"], "C",
 "One-to-one pools give each internal host its own external address with no port translation, so when every address is taken, new hosts cannot be translated."),
("Which IP pool type gives each user a block of source ports to cut down NAT logging?",
 ["Overload", "One-to-one", "Fixed port range", "Port block allocation"], "D",
 "Port block allocation hands each internal user a block of ports on a pool address, so one log entry covers the whole block. Fixed port range maps a set internal range by a fixed formula."),
("A VIP maps 203.0.113.10 to 10.0.0.10. What else is needed for inbound traffic to reach the server?",
 ["A policy using the VIP", "A static route to the VIP", "An IP pool for the VIP", "Nothing else"], "A",
 "A VIP only defines the translation. A firewall policy from the external to the internal interface with the VIP as destination must allow the traffic."),
("A VIP forwards external TCP 8080 to the server's port 80. What does an Internet client connect to?",
 ["Internal IP, port 80", "External IP, port 8080", "External IP, port 80", "Internal IP, port 8080"], "B",
 "The client uses the VIP's external address and external port; FortiGate rewrites both to the mapped internal IP and port 80."),
("With central NAT enabled, where is source NAT configured?",
 ["On each firewall policy", "On the egress interface", "In the central SNAT table", "In each VIP object"], "C",
 "Central NAT removes the NAT setting from firewall policies; source NAT rules live in the central SNAT table instead."),
("With central NAT enabled, what destination does a policy use for traffic to a VIP?",
 ["The VIP object name", "The VIP external IP", "The VIP's external port", "The mapped internal IP"], "D",
 "With central NAT, destination NAT happens before the policy lookup, so the policy's destination is the real internal address rather than the VIP."),
("In diagnose sys session list output, which entry shows that source NAT was applied?",
 ["act=snat", "act=dnat", "act=noop", "act=accept"], "A",
 "A hook=post line with act=snat shows the translated source address and port. act=dnat appears when a VIP rewrote the destination."),
("Fixed port is enabled on a NAT policy. What changes?",
 ["The destination port is kept", "The source port is kept", "Port 0 is always used", "Ports are randomized"], "B",
 "Fixed port keeps the original source port, which some protocols need. It also limits how many sessions can share one translated IP."),
("Internal users must reach a server by its public VIP address from inside. What must exist?",
 ["A second VIP on the LAN", "A route to the public IP", "A LAN policy using the VIP", "An IP pool on the LAN side"], "C",
 "For hairpin access, the VIP must apply on the internal side (external interface any or the LAN) and a policy from the LAN must use the VIP as its destination."),
("Which VIP setting makes FortiGate answer ARP requests for the external address?",
 ["nat-source-vip", "portforward", "extintf", "arp-reply"], "D",
 "arp-reply, enabled by default, makes FortiGate respond to ARP for the VIP's external address so the upstream router can reach it. extintf only sets the interface the VIP listens on."),
("An IP pool uses addresses outside the egress interface subnet. What lets return traffic reach FortiGate?",
 ["An upstream route", "Disabling arp-reply", "A VIP per address", "A local policy route"], "A",
 "FortiGate's ARP replies only help on the connected subnet, so a pool on another subnet needs the upstream router to route that range to FortiGate."),
("A deny policy uses destination all. By default, does it block traffic to a VIP?",
 ["Yes, all includes VIPs", "No, needs match-vip", "Only for TCP traffic", "Only with central NAT"], "B",
 "Deny policies skip VIP traffic unless set match-vip enable is configured, because the destination is matched after translation. Using the VIP itself as destination also works."),
("Which IP pool type maps an internal range to an external range so translations can be calculated without session logs?",
 ["Overload", "One-to-one", "Fixed port range", "Port block allocation"], "C",
 "Fixed port range ties each internal address to a set external address and port range by formula, so the translation can be worked out later. Port block allocation still logs each block."),
("How are central SNAT rules evaluated?",
 ["Most specific rule first", "By lowest rule ID", "Last matching rule", "Top down, first match"], "D",
 "Central SNAT rules are read from the top, and the first rule matching the traffic applies, just like firewall policies."),
]
assert len(ITEMS) == 16
for i, it in enumerate(ITEMS):
    assert it[2] == "ABCD"[i % 4], i
    assert len(set(it[1])) == 4, i
